      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To find the remainder when the sum \\(1 + 7 + 13 + 19 + \\cdots + 253 + 259\\) is divided by 6, we first need to determine the number of terms in the sequence. The sequence is an arithmetic progression (AP) with the first term \\(a = 1\\) and common difference \\(d = 6\\).\n\nThe \\(n\\)-th term of an arithmetic sequence is given by:\n\\[a_n = a + (n-1)d\\]\nWe know the last term \\(a_n = 259\\). Setting up the equation, we get:\n\\[259 = 1 + (n-1) \\cdot 6\\]\n\\[259 = 1 + 6n - 6\\]\n\\[259 = 6n - 5\\]\n\\[264 = 6n\\]\n\\[n = 44\\]\nSo, there are 44 terms in the sequence.\n\nThe sum \\(S_n\\) of the first \\(n\\) terms of an arithmetic sequence is given by:\n\\[S_n = \\frac{n}{2} (a + l)\\]\nwhere \\(l\\) is the last term. Substituting the known values, we get:\n\\[S_{44} = \\frac{44}{2} (1 + 259) = 22 \\cdot 260 = 5720\\]\n\nNow, we need to find the remainder when 5720 is divided by 6. We do this by finding the remainder of each term in the sequence when divided by 6 and then summing these remainders.\n\nThe remainders of the first few terms when divided by 6 are:\n\\[1 \\div 6 \\quad \\text{remainder} \\quad 1\\]\n\\[7 \\div 6 \\quad \\text{remainder} \\quad 1\\]\n\\[13 \\div 6 \\quad \\text{remainder} \\quad 1\\]\n\\[19 \\div 6 \\quad \\text{remainder} \\quad 1\\]\nand so on. We see that every term in the sequence leaves a remainder of 1 when divided by 6.\n\nTherefore, the sum of these remainders is:\n\\[44 \\cdot 1 = 44\\]\nNow, we find the remainder when 44 is divided by 6:\n\\[44 \\div 6 = 7 \\text{ remainder } 2\\]\nSo, the remainder when 5720 is divided by 6 is 2.\n\nThus, the remainder when the sum \\(1 + 7 + 13 + 19 + \\cdots + 253 + 259\\) is divided by 6 is \\(\\boxed{2}\\).",
